  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 92,
    "conclusion": "Site A’s faster order completion is partly caused by the new picking scanner, but the scanner is not the primary or sole explanation. Randomized crossover evidence estimates a modest scanner benefit of about 4% (95% CI: 1–7%), corroborated by robustness estimates near 3–5%. However, Site A was already about 3% faster before rollout, and a residual advantage of roughly 3–6% persists after alternative exclusions, order-mix adjustments, and matched-worker analyses. The observed advantage also varies by operating conditions—about 5–9%, largest for high-complexity orders—supporting retained explanations involving site-specific processes, capabilities, or interactions with order complexity. Reject a scanner-only explanation and any claim that the full raw 11% post-rollout gap was caused by the scanner. Retain the scanner as a meaningful partial driver, alongside pre-existing and operational site differences whose precise causes are not identified by the available evidence."
  },
